Include at least one mathematical formula in the paper, such as a gate score,
expected utility, crossover criterion, or planned statistical test. The formula
should support the review or prediction logic rather than pretending to report
actual measurements.

## Required Paper Work

Write and compile:

```text
latex/template.tex
latex/template.pdf
latex/template.docx
```

Use an AI Scientist-v2-style manuscript structure rather than a one-page
artifact stub. The main paper should look like a serious workshop submission:

- title, abstract, introduction, related work, background, method, experimental
  setup, predicted results, limitations, conclusion, and references;
- enough substantive prose to explain the problem, design choices, protocol,
  predicted evidence, and caveats (roughly 2,000+ words unless the idea is
  unusually narrow);
- real, relevant citations in `latex/references.bib` and `\cite{...}` calls in
  the manuscript;
- at least one professional table and at least one generated figure;
- an impact/ethics or deployment-risk paragraph when the domain affects safety,
  infrastructure, maintenance, or public services;
- a concise keyword line near the abstract, using ASCII translations when
  necessary for pdflatex compatibility;
- an appendix or supplementary section when details do not fit in the main text.

Borrow the paper organization discipline from AI Scientist-v2, but preserve this
repository's low-compute rule: do not run experiments and do not report predicted
values as measured results.

Also borrow AI Scientist-v2's strongest non-experimental habits: ideation
discipline, journal-to-report continuity, real citation collection, top-tier
reviewer self-critique, VLM-style figure/caption auditing, complete venue-style
paper structure, and explicit comparisons against strong baselines. Keep this
repository's AI Scientist-v3 advantages: Claude/Harbor execution, lightweight
instructions, recoverable artifacts, strict review gates, versioned snapshots,
and low orchestration overhead.
When Claude-compatible subagents are available, use them for review: comprehensive
review, idea/literature review, protocol/reproducibility review, figure/caption
review, and a final area-chair gate with the harshest top-A-conference standard.

Use the paper to present:

- the research question and motivation,
- relevant related work,
- the proposed experimental protocol,
- the rationality review findings,
- clearly labeled predicted results,
- the predicted data table and at least one generated figure,
- at least one formula defining the review or prediction criterion,
- recommended changes before running,
- limitations caused by not executing experiments,
- future empirical evidence required before making performance claims.

Frame the manuscript protocol-first. The title, abstract, introduction, and
conclusion must lead with the pre-registered evaluation contract; a named detector
is only a candidate instantiation used to stress-test that contract. If a visible
forecast table is condensed for page fit, the body must say it is a selected
subset of `predicted_results/predicted_results.csv`, name the selection rule, and
point to the CSV as the authoritative full comparison surface.

Also generate `manuscript_explanation.md` as a separate companion note. It should
explain that the PDF is a polished manuscript-effect test/protocol draft, point
to `predicted_results/predicted_results.csv`, summarize the evidence-status and
calibration fields, and list the replacement triggers for future real training
and benchmark execution. The PDF itself should still avoid false empirical
claims, but it can use normal scientific table names and visual styling.

Add a dedicated comparative evaluation plan. It must cover same-family
baselines, stronger detector baselines, crack-specific segmentation baselines,
transformer detectors when appropriate, ablations, matched budgets,
hard-negative sets, leakage controls, uncertainty estimates, and stop/go
criteria. If a baseline is infeasible in the low-compute setting, state that it
is planned for future execution rather than omitting it.

Add a dedicated top-tier gap analysis. It should answer: what would a strong
NeurIPS/ICLR/CVPR-style reviewer still reject, what evidence is missing, which
claims are not yet supported, and what artifacts would be needed to close the
gap. Be strict and specific.

The paper must clearly say that no new experiments were run. It may include a
predicted-results section or table with expected rankings, trends, or rough
ranges, as long as the text labels them as predicted/hypothesized and explains the
rationale. Do not include fake measured accuracy, runtime, statistical
significance, figures, or measured results. Tables may summarize planned
baselines, datasets, metrics, validity risks, decision criteria, and predicted
outcomes, but not pretend outcomes.
Avoid the word "fake" in the manuscript. A strong paper should make the
estimated-result table look like a normal scientific artifact while making the
evidence status unambiguous: protocol-estimated, literature-calibrated, not yet
measured, and designed to be replaced by future execution.
